 Introduction
 **********

 The ever-growing presence of abusive content on social media is a significant concern, seriously impacting the safety and well-being of users. A wide range of harmful techniques has been employed to spread such content, making it particularly challenging for platforms to manually detect. To overcome these difficulties, researchers have developed and shared datasets containing annotated abusive content, facilitating the creation and evaluation of advanced machine learning models. Our work contributes to these efforts by comparing the predictive capabilities of various models, including both classical and deep learning architectures, in the prediction of abusive content. \cite{DBLP:noauthor2018hierarchical} 

 Related Works
 ***********

 Existing research indicates that deep learning models perform better than classical models in the prediction of abusive content. \cite{DBLP:noauthor2018hierarchical, DBLP:varsen2018classifying} A recent study also demonstrated the effectiveness of pre-trained multi-lingual language models in this context. \cite{DBLP:varsen2018classifying} However, to our knowledge, no research has been conducted on the application of transformer architecture in the prediction of abusive content. Our work applies and evaluates, for the first time, various transformer-based models, including both encoder-decoder and transformer with recurrent and recurrent architectures, in the prediction of abusive content. \cite{DBLP:noauthor2018hierarchical} 

